## What the Lean kernel checks

Polynomial identities over an arbitrary commutative ring `R` (`Lean.Grind.CommRing R`), in the 25 symbols
`mij` (i, j = 0..3; the 4x4 matrix M), `f0..f3`, `h0..h3`, `s`.

    a_j  = cofactor of M[j][3]            (a 3x3 determinant with sign (-1)^(j+3))
    detM = sum_j M[j][3] * a_j            (expansion along the last column)
    kk_l = sum_j M[j][l] * f_j            (kk = M^T f),  l = 0, 1, 2 (kk_3 is not a generator)
    e_j  = f_j * h3 - s * a_j
    F    = f^T M h

* `detM_first_row` : detM equals the expansion of det M along the first row (so `detM` is the determinant).
* `I5`  : sum_j M[j][l] * a_j = 0 for l = 0, 1, 2.
* `I1`  : F - s * detM = h0*kk0 + h1*kk1 + h2*kk2 + sum_j M[j][3] * e_j.
  So the hypersurface {F - s detM = 0} contains the scheme {e_j = 0, kk_l = 0}.
* `I2_l`: h3 * kk_l = sum_j M[j][l] * e_j   (l = 0, 1, 2): where h3 is invertible, the kk_l are in the ideal (e).
* `I3_ij`: a_i * f_j - a_j * f_i = +- det3 (the 3x3 matrix P without column i, column j replaced by (kk0,kk1,kk2)),
  for the six pairs i < j: so a_i f_j - a_j f_i lies in the ideal (kk0, kk1, kk2).
* `I4_ij`: f_i * e_j - f_j * e_i = s * (a_i * f_j - a_j * f_i).
* `cramer_i` (i = 0..3): detM * f_i = sum_l C[i][l] * kk_l with C the cofactor matrix (kk_3 included): so
  detM * (f) is contained in (kk_0..kk_3), one inclusion of the Northcott-Wiebe colon formula used in section 5B.
* `F_in_kk`: F = h0*kk0 + h1*kk1 + h2*kk2 + h3*kk3: so F lies in the ideal of R = V(kk, detM).

## What is NOT checked here

Everything geometric: degrees and homogeneity, the local structure of the family, flatness, the closure, the
liaison chain, the cycle classes, the statement about Hodge loci. Those are ordinary mathematics in PROOF_G.md
sections 5A and 5B (written by an AI, refereed only by AI agents). The converse inclusion of the
Northcott-Wiebe formula is a cited theorem, not checked here. The case of general c is NOT covered by this file
(sympy checks c = 2..5: night/gen_ci/gen_family_check.py).
